<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.5.3: Factoring Trinomials Using Trial and Error</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01652</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.5.3: Factoring Trinomials Using Trial and Error</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7fa7f4ed-0aa3-4612-b4b1-ad1cfb88d884</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity introduces students to a new way to factor trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\), where the leading
  coefficient is not \(1\). Students will use trial and error to find the binomial factors.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Introduce the trial and error process used to factor a trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\). Explain this is
  similar to the Undoing FOIL process, but that it is made more complicated when the leading coefficient is not \(1\). </para>
<para>Assign students partners to help them talk through the process of using trial and error to factor trinomials.</para>
<para>Read the helpful rules as a class. Discuss each one.</para>
<para>Allow each pair of students to work together to solve the remaining problems. Encourage them to share their reasoning
  as they use trial and error to factor the trinomials.</para>
<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for English Language Learners</title><para><!--Extra Support Name-->MLR 5 Co-Craft Questions: Speaking, Reading</para>
  <!--Support Content-->
    <para>Use this routine to help students talk through the trial and error process to factor trinomials. Ask students to write down possible mathematical questions that could be asked to help them determine which factors could solve the problem. Invite students to compare their questions before starting the trial and error process. Listen for and amplify any questions involving factoring trinomials. The process of creating mathematical questions, without the pressure of producing answers or solutions, prompts students to make sense of the given information and to activate the language of mathematical questions. This work helps to prepare students to process the actual questions. </para>
    <para><emphasis effect="italics"><!--Support Italics-->Design Principle(s): Maximize meta-awareness; Support sense-making</emphasis></para><para><!--Extra Support Title-->Learn more about this routine</para>
    <!-- Extra Support Content-->
    <para><link url="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P_NQJdG92iA;&amp;rel=0">View the instructional video</link> and <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cf9c18a54843b1501c625523ff7db865c3249d5e">follow along with the materials</link> to assist you with learning this routine. </para>
    <para><!--Extra Support Title-->Provide support for students</para>
    <!-- Extra Support Content-->
    <para><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4e1aaa71f3967a980f03bc41217282931099c703">Distribute graphic organizers</link> to the students to assist them with participating in this routine. </para>
  </note>

<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title><para><!--Extra Support Name-->Action and Expression:  Flexibility and Choice</para>
  <!--Support Content-->
    <para>Provide students with algebra tiles to help factoring by trial and error.   Algebra tiles help represent algebraic equations and provide students with a visualization of the process of factoring polynomials and trinomials. </para>
    <para> Watch the video in Factoring Trinomials with Leading Coefficients of 1 to see an example of how to use algebra tiles at the 2:00 mark. </para>
    <para><emphasis effect="italics"><!--Support Italics-->Supports accessibility for Conceptual processing, Visual-Spatial Processing</emphasis></para></note>

<title>Student Activity </title>
<para><emphasis>How to factor trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) using trial and error.</emphasis></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - 
  </emphasis>Write the trinomial in descending order of degrees as needed.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - 
  </emphasis>Factor any GCF.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - 
  </emphasis>Find all the factor pairs of the first term.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - 
  </emphasis>Find all the factor pairs of the third term.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Test all the possible combinations of the factors until the correct product is found.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> Check by multiplying.</para>
<para><emphasis>Remember these helpful rules when factoring trinomials:</emphasis></para>
<list>
  <item> Factor out the GCF first.
    <list>
      <item>This is an important rule
        since it makes the remaining trinomial much simpler to factor. It may
        change the trinomial from the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) to the form \(x^2 +
        bx + c\). This would allow you to use the “undoing FOIL” process
        instead of trial and error.</item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>When the middle term is negative and the last term is positive, the signs in the binomials must both be negative.
    <list>
      <item>Only two negative numbers will result in a negative sum and a positive product.</item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>If the leading coefficient is negative, so is the GCF.
    <list>
      <item>Factoring out the negative sign from the leading coefficient will make the trinomial simpler to factor.</item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>When the leading term is not 1
    and there is no GCF, then the first two terms of the binomial factors
    will multiply to equal the leading term.
    <list>
      <item>This means you can use trial and error to find the correct binomial factors. </item>
    </list>
  </item>
</list>
<para>Work with a partner. Factor each trinomial. Ask your partner if you
  get stuck. Compare your answers and discuss any issues you had.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(2a^2 + 5a + 3\) 
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((a + 1)(2a + 3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(8x^2 − 14x + 3\) 
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((2x − 3)(4x − 1)\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(18x^2 − 3xy − 10y^2\) 
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((3x + 2y)(6x − 5y)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(15n^3 − 85n^2 + 100n\) 
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(5n(n − 4)(3n − 5)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(−10y^4 − 55y^3 − 60y^2\) 
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(−5y^2(y + 4)(2y + 3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(56q^3 + 320q^2 − 96q\)
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(8q(q + 6)(7q − 2)\) </para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite students to provide suggestions for how to factor a trinomial of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\).</para>
<list>
  <item> Undo FOIL </item>
</list>
<para>Invite students to provide suggestions for how to factor a trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\).</para>
<list>
  <item> First, check to see if a GCF can be factored out from the trinomial. </item>
  <list>
    <item> If yes, and a trinomial of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\) remains, then “Undo FOIL.” </item>
    <item> If yes or no, and a trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\) remains, try trial and error. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>Ask a student to summarize in their own words the steps used when factoring a trinomial using trial and error.
  Display the steps shown at the start of the activity.</para>
<para>Then review the rules for factoring using trial and error that were introduced at the start of the activity. Ask
  students if there is any point of confusion regarding these rules for factoring trinomials.</para>
<title>6.5.3: Self Check</title>
<para><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para>QUESTION:</para>
<para>Factor the trinomial completely: \(6y^3 + 18y^2 − 60y\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>ANSWERS</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>FEEDBACK</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(6y(y + 2)(y − 5)\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The middle term is positive, so the greater number in the
          binomials should be added, not subtracted. The correct answer is \(6y(y − 2)(y + 5)\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\((6y^2 − 12y)(y + 5)\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The answer is not completely factored. The first step is to
          factor out the GCF of 6y. The correct answer is \(6y(y − 2)(y + 5)\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(6y(y − 2)(y + 5)\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>That’s correct! Check yourself: Multiply the factors together to determine if you have \(6y^3 + 18y^2
          − 60y\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(6y(y + 2)(y + 5)\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The last term of the trinomials is negative, so the
          binomials should have opposite signs. After removing the GCF of \(6y\), the trinomial is not correctly
          factored. The correct answer is \(6y(y − 2)(y + 5)\).</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>6.5.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with
  the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with
  those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Factoring Trinomials Using Trial and Error</title>
<para>Our next step is to factor trinomials whose leading coefficient is not \(1\), trinomials of the form \(ax^2 + bx +
  c\). Remember to always check for a GCF first! Sometimes, after you factor the GCF, the leading coefficient of the
  trinomial becomes \(1\) and you can factor it by the methods we’ve used so far. Let’s do an example to see
  how this works.</para>
<para>Example 1</para>
<para>Factor completely: \(4x^3 + 16x^2 − 20x\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - 
  </emphasis>The trinomial is already written in descending order of degrees.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Is there a greatest common factor?</para>
<para>  \(4x^3+16x^2−20x\)
  Yes, \(GCF=4x\). Factor it.
  \(4x(x^2+4x−5)\)</para>
<para> Is it a binomial, trinomial, or more than three terms?
  \(4x(x^2+4x−5)\) 
  It is now a trinomial with a leading coefficient of \(1\). So “undo FOIL.”
  \(4x (x    )(x    )\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Use a table like the one shown to find two numbers that multiply to \(−5\) and add to \(4\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Factors of \(−5\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Sum of factors</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\(-1,5\) </entry>
      <entry>\(−1+5=4^ \ast\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(1,-5\) </entry>
      <entry>\(1+(−5)=−4\) </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Use −1 and 5 as coefficients of the last terms.</para>
<para>  \(4x(x−1)(x+5)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check.</para>
<para>
  <m:math display="inline">
    <m:semantics>
      <m:mrow>
        <m:mrow>
          <m:mspace/>
          <m:mtable>
            <m:mtr>
              <m:mtd>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo>(</m:mo>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
